WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.350
Film Courage: Just off camera you told us
that you go to Meetup groups for writers.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.630
And I’m wondering what Meetup groups strike
your eye in terms of why you go to it and

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.440
what do you get out of it?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:18.450
David Willis: Well, when I was first starting
writing, I had some people help and give me

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.740
some advice.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:26.070
The first writers group that I went to (that
I was a part of), it was all sitcom comedy

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:34.489
writing and the very first note I ever got
on my first TV script was “This was so boring,

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:35.690
I was falling asleep.”

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:47.309
And we all laughed because comedy writers
can be pretty vicious in a funny way.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:50.809
And then he said when he got to this part
that’s when I woke up and it was really

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:51.809
funny.

00:00:51.809 --> 00:01:00.839
So this group showed me right away that you
need honest feedback but constructive criticism.

00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:06.670
Because this particular writer, he gave me
that note but then he told me what was working

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.100
so I go “You know, you’re right.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.930
I feel the same thing, I agree with the note.”

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.570
So it really helped me out.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.290
That group (that little writer’s group I
was in) eventually dissolved because everyone

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.290
got work.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:22.450
That’s a great reason for a group to dissolve,
everyone was working on one of those shows.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.579
So I thought what was really helpful when
I was first starting out.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:27.820
I thought “I’d like to help people, too.”

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.649
So I go to the Meetup site and I see that
there’s a writer’s group and so I go to

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:33.210
these and I just give them notes.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.869
You know, gentle notes, encouraging notes,
but honest notes.

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:41.750
And you know it’s helpful to people.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:50.899
And you know, if you don’t ever give something
back or help other people, that doesn’t

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:51.899
feel very good.

00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:52.899
So…

00:01:52.899 --> 00:01:55.659
Film Courage: Okay, so that’s your way of
giving back.

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:01.319
What do you see (and I won’t ask you 5 things),
but what do you see as several top things

00:02:01.319 --> 00:02:04.850
that most new writers have trouble with?

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.940
David Willis: Well the two big things are
1) is passive lead character.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.190
I see that quite a bit.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.910
I see it over and over.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.941
Their lead character has a bunch of people
coming at them and doing things “Oh you

00:02:18.941 --> 00:02:19.941
should do this.”

00:02:19.941 --> 00:02:20.941
And he goes “Okay!”

00:02:20.941 --> 00:02:21.941
And he does it.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:26.770
If it doesn’t come from within the character,
then you’ve got no train…you’ve got

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.320
no choo-choo pulling the train, it’s not
pulling the story train.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.130
What you’ve got is a caboose pushing it.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.130
It doesn’t work, okay?

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.420
You need pull it and have it go somewhere.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.000
And I see that a lot, just with these passive
lead characters.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:49.110
The other thing is either no conflict or very
lowercase conflict where (same as TV), people

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.970
are flipping through channels and they watch
something for five, six seconds.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:03:00.650
If there isn’t deep conflict that is popping
off the screen, they’re going to keep flipping.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.790
They are flipping past your show, okay!

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:07.410
So you like conflict, you know?

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.240
When you watch TV, put it in your script.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.700
One of the reasons it is difficult is because
in real life, people don’t like conflict.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.950
If you are a nice person, you don’t want
to yell and argue with people.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.920
You don’t want to hurt people’s feelings,
okay?

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.020
But in real life that is great.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.820
In a script, that’s boring!

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.400
That’s just really boring.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.730
So let the conflict go.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:37.150
Let is go into that script, put it in there
and then it will jump off the page when people

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.180
read it.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.340
Passive lead character and not enough conflict.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:52.510
I see it all the time and the other thing
that I see is…people are safe.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:59.210
I have a professional comedy writers group
and people apply to it and we’ve had like

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:04.470
a thousand people apply in this group and
there is only 20 people in it so we always

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.930
just like certain people to be in it.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:11.271
Most of whom are professional or they are
show runners or they write for shows and if

00:04:11.271 --> 00:04:14.420
they are newer writers, they are good and
they are on their way.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.940
And I get people applying to the group, you
know perfectly nice people, people who send

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.890
their script and they have screenwriting degrees,
you know?

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:27.810
Sometimes multiple degrees in writing and
theater and everything in their script happens

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.760
at exactly the right time.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.930
The inciting incident, the first act break,
the midpoint, the third act, and all of these

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.520
story threads are “perfectly correct.”

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:44.080
And I don’t give a crap because they were
so busy being correct in their form, they

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.500
haven’t put any spark of madness into the
script, they haven’t put anything quirky

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.100
and personal, you know?

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.100
It’s not in there.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.210
It’s like forget all this stuff you learn
and just let it pour out.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:05.200
Just allow your craziness, your weirdness,
your quirkiness to be poured into that script.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.420
That’s the other mistake is I see people,
they are holding back.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:09.890
This is show business!

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:15.290
Okay, this is the one business where there
is nothing wrong, okay.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:19.540
You can let these characters say weird things,
crazy things, politically incorrect things,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.570
mean things, stupid things, anything!

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:27.950
You have the license to do an infinite number
of things, do it!

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.550
Film Courage: Okay.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:37.980
So speaking of conflict, let’s say the conflict
was not in the script, but when you gave a

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:38.980
note.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.690
Was there ever conflict and was there ever
a time alternatively where you thought there

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.540
would be (conflict) and you were scared and
actually the person thanked you?

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.930
David Willis: Yeah, that’s happened.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.080
I mean there’s a way to give a note.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.740
Your job when you give notes is to help the
writer tell the story they’re trying to

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:56.740
tell.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.210
To help them tell it better.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:04.090
You say “Hey, this character is coming off
as this…is that what you meant?

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.090
And they are like “No…no…no.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:06.090
I meant for…”

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.540
And you go “Well, it’s coming off as this…”
and you tell them and they’re grateful.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:13.700
“Oh, I didn’t realize that?”

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:19.210
From little things like I was giving notes
to one person and he described a character

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.110
as elderly, this elderly character.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.750
And then (in the next paragraph) he called
him 50 years old.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:26.750
Film Courage: [Laughs].

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.110
David Willis: Yes, you get it.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.110
You’re laughing.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.240
And a lot of people at the table were (you
know) around 50.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:37.980
And so I said “You know, when you say ‘elderly’
we’re thinking…and you say 50.”

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.680
And he goes (and he was a very young writer)
and he says “Wasn’t that, isn’t that

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.680
elderly?”

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.900
And like all the people around him were like
“No!!!!!”

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.020
The reason this matters is because A) It’s
a little confusing.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.020
You want clarity in the script.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:51.020
And B)

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:56.410
If somebody who is reading this, who could
hire you is 50 and you call them elderly,

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.680
it’s just…why create trouble, right?

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.540
So there’s little notes like that.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:06.680
But I was working on a show, I came to work
one day and there was a writing who was walking

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:11.900
out with their stuff in a box and they had
just been let go.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.610
And it was like “Ooohhh?”

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:17.199
And we didn’t ask the executive producer
anything.

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:20.540
But he said and he was a really nice guy.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:29.490
“She didn’t know when to stop defending
a pitch.”

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.650
In the room she was a perfectly nice woman,
everybody liked her.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.400
But when you’re in the writer’s room in
a sitcom, there’s a bunch of writers there

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.400
and you’ll pitch an idea.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.320
And the executive will say “Oh, we need
this in this scene.”

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.400
And you’ll pitch an idea and the executive
producer will say “Oh, yeah, that’s great.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.400
Write that.”

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.840
Or they will say “Oh, almost…not quite."

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.870
And other people will pitch in and then somebody
will pitch in and there will be like 3 or

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.110
4 pitches later based on your pitch, you will
come up with a solution in the script and

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:54.330
you write it.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.360
Or the executive producer will say “No!

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.360
You should move on.”

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.650
Okay, because it’s their job to know what’s
in the show.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:03.770
The executive producer says that.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.960
And you don’t want to have discussions about
it.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.180
And she kept defending.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.550
She kept going back to it and saying “Well,
you know if you did that thing I say.”

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:18.630
And it’s like, it just wastes time and it
makes the executive producer feel bad because

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.030
they’ve got to keep saying “No.”

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.120
So he eventually just let her go.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:31.430
So I learned from that, that was in my career
when you are given a note, like “No!”

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:34.099
By your boss, okay?

00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:36.180
Take the note.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.050
Also sometimes what’s called, I don’t
know if you’ve heard the concept ‘the-note-behind-the-note?’

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:45.470
And sometimes there is something under the
note that is not clear, so you have to clarify

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.579
the note for somebody, you know.

00:08:48.579 --> 00:08:52.389
But it’s your job (and you’re a professional),
it’s your job to explain the note until

00:08:52.389 --> 00:08:58.329
you see a light go on in their eyes, that’s
your job to make them understand the note.

00:08:58.329 --> 00:09:01.779
And maybe your note is a great note but it’s
not the right note for the story they are

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:03.290
trying to.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:07.420
But when I’m working at these Meetups with
newer writers and stuff, you take into account

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:09.319
that they are new, you know?

00:09:09.319 --> 00:09:15.670
And I’ve seen a million scripts and I’ve
solved a lot of script problems and they haven’t

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:16.670
seen those yet.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:21.860
So you are just trying to say “Hey, this
problem in your script has been solved in

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.800
a couple of ways by doing this and this.”

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:26.050
And how does that feel?

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.819
And when you are giving the note you can’t
be attached to the note if it’s not a professional

00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:32.690
situation where if you’re not their boss
or whatever, you can’t be attached to it.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:33.990
You can’t get upset if they don’t take
your note.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.110
I mean, it’s not about you, okay?

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.660
It’s about the writer and their script.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:43.660
There are some really fun writers at these
Meetup groups.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:49.639
There are very talented people of all ages
and it’s great that they can come together

00:09:49.639 --> 00:09:51.600
and see their stuff come to life.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.860
Film Courage: It’s great that the over 50
ones can actually get out of the house!

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.471
I’m so glad they can make it there.

00:09:58.471 --> 00:10:02.289
David Willis: I’ve written and I can’t
get up!

